# =============================================================================
# Section 27's straightened C/H with the OUTER LAYER'S RADIALS CURVED: 2 layers,
# 32 chordwise blocks per surface, 8 wake stretches, and the outer radial
# declared as an arc that bends outward instead of running as a straight chord.
# 176 blocks, 28640 cells, 0 inverted.
#
# WHY. The maintainer read fig6 as perpendicular NEAR the body and curving into
# arcs further out, and section 28's profile measurement says this repo's meshes
# do the opposite: splitting each wall-normal line's turning into quarters of its
# own length gives 0.34 / 0.00 / 0.00 / 0.01 degrees for section 27, all of it
# against the wall and then dead straight. At blend 0.5 it is 2.22 / 0.74 / 0.50
# / 0.59 -- the turning continues outward, which is the shape asked for.
#
# WHAT IT COSTS: non-orthogonality MEAN 1.840 -> 2.980 deg, worst corner
# 50.06 -> 51.65. Section 27's case is kept unchanged beside this one because
# that trade is a choice, not an improvement.
#
# NOT GRIDPRO'S MECHANISM. Its arcs are what a converged elliptic solve leaves
# over straight wireframe links; here the arc is DECLARED. Section 28 records
# that the smoother was tried and cannot do it -- it adds turning only against
# the wall and folds 1000 cells at 60 iterations.
#
#   python3 docs/research/gridpro-blocking-method/make_ch.py \
#       --layers 2 --split 32 --turn-blend 0.8 --wake 8 \
#       --arc-out --arc-out-blend 0.5 --out gridpro_ch_arcout.json
#   ./run.sh -conf docs/research/gridpro-blocking-method/gridpro_ch_arcout.dat
#
# THE BLEND VALUE IS A CHOICE, NOT A MATCH. Converted to pixels at fig6's own
# scale, the sagitta inside the only window it publishes is 0.03 px for the
# straight case and 0.86 px here -- a sub-pixel difference on a compressed image
# whose grid lines are 2 px wide. fig5 contains the whole line but at 86 px per
# chord (1.5 px here) and is a different geometry. So any blend from 0 to 1
# looks identical in every vendor image; 0.5 was picked because it is visible in
# THIS repo's rendering. Section 28e. gridpro_ch_straight.dat beside this one is
# the defensible default: better non-orthogonality, and the difference this file
# buys cannot be seen in any source.
#
# A RESEARCH HARNESS, not a shipped case.
#
MESH_MODE 1
MESH_TOPOLOGY_FILE docs/research/gridpro-blocking-method/gridpro_ch_arcout.json

# The aerofoil the C-grid case already ships: two segments, upper and lower,
# split at the leading edge because that is where a block corner sits. There is
# NO far-field geometry -- the rectangle is declared, corner by corner.
GEOM_FILE examples/geometries/naca0012_cgrid.dat

BL_INITIAL_THICKNESS 0.001
MB_SPLIT_QUADS 1

# The FALLBACK, and here it is load bearing: the far-field and outlet edges
# carry no binding, so this is where their condition comes from. The aerofoil's
# sides are bound and take 'wall' off their own sidecar. A real case would want
# inlet / outlet / farfield told apart, which needs a far-field geometry file.
BC_GEOM farfield

# The repo default of 20 sweeps DIVERGES on this family (section 8g); five is
# where the wall height is held and nothing has turned.
MB_SMOOTH_ITERS 5

EXPORT_VTK 1
OUTPUT_FILENAME results/meshes/gridpro_ch_arcout/mesh_gridpro_ch_arcout.vtk
